# What is historically solid

The historical skeleton is credible.

Geoffrey Samuel explicitly describes early Śaiva transgressive traditions involving cremation grounds, substances linked with death and sexuality, and fierce gods and goddesses. He also proposes a broad historical movement in which many external practices were later dropped or replaced by internal yogic processes involving subtle-body physiology. ([Cambridge University Press & Assessment][2])

The existence of Kāpālika or skull-bearing Śaiva ascetics is not merely a modern media invention. The evidence is uneven—inscriptions, literary sources, polemics and representations rather than practitioner memoirs—but there is a real historical subject beneath the caricature. ([De Gruyter Brill][3])

Your formulation:

> **family resemblance, not proven unbroken identity**

is exactly the right way to handle the Kāpālika–Aghori relationship. Keep it.

Your evidence-board distinction between:

* documented;
* plausible;
* hagiographic;
* misleading;

is one of the strongest parts of the entire blueprint. Turn it into a mandatory schema used by every episode.

